WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.340
A newborn rat muscle fiber is wired to two or more motor neurons.

2
00:00:08.060 --> 00:00:09.980
Nothing is wrong with the extra inputs.

3
00:00:10.460 --> 00:00:14.440
Each one releases acetylcholine, and each one makes the fiber respond.

4
00:00:15.120 --> 00:00:18.860
In the adult, each fiber has one junction and one axon.

5
00:00:19.380 --> 00:00:22.260
Within two to three weeks, the fiber listens to just one.

6
00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:24.180
Where do the extra inputs go?

7
00:00:24.600 --> 00:00:26.140
And who decides which one stays?

8
00:00:27.133 --> 00:00:29.513
First, did the extra neurons die?

9
00:00:29.973 --> 00:00:31.053
That would explain the loss.

10
00:00:31.613 --> 00:00:36.433
But in rat soleus muscle, the number of motor neurons did not change significantly.

11
00:00:36.913 --> 00:00:40.993
Yet by the end of the second week, nearly every fiber had one input.

12
00:00:41.733 --> 00:00:46.413
Both facts are true only if each surviving neuron pulled back some of its branches.

13
00:00:46.413 --> 00:00:49.773
Losing an input means losing a branch, not a cell.

14
00:00:50.517 --> 00:00:53.837
Now watch one junction over days, in Mousepups.

15
00:00:54.377 --> 00:00:58.897
Each axon glows in its own color, and the receptor sites are labeled too.

16
00:00:59.597 --> 00:01:02.237
Typically, one axon withdraws from the sites.

17
00:01:02.837 --> 00:01:05.017
The other extends into the sites it left.

18
00:01:05.557 --> 00:01:06.997
The movie looks like a takeover.

19
00:01:07.517 --> 00:01:12.077
And the winner could not be reliably predicted from how much territory each axon

20
00:01:12.077 --> 00:01:13.077
held at the start.

21
00:01:13.700 --> 00:01:15.220
So what shapes the contest?

22
00:01:16.640 --> 00:01:21.680
In newborn rat muscle, blocking nerve impulses slows elimination.

23
00:01:22.320 --> 00:01:24.720
Stimulating the muscle sometimes speeds it up.

24
00:01:25.040 --> 00:01:28.480
But these tests changed activity everywhere at once.

25
00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:33.160
They could not tell whether the loser is silent, or only less active than its

26
00:01:33.160 --> 00:01:34.160
neighbor.

27
00:01:34.333 --> 00:01:36.073
Here is a test, in adult mice.

28
00:01:36.733 --> 00:01:41.573
A toxin, alpha -bungaro toxin, binds acetylcholine receptors and blocks them.

29
00:01:42.033 --> 00:01:43.313
Block one patch of a junction.

30
00:01:43.873 --> 00:01:48.013
The receptors there are lost, and the nerve terminal withdraws from that patch.

31
00:01:48.473 --> 00:01:49.813
Now block the whole junction.

32
00:01:50.293 --> 00:01:51.293
Nothing is eliminated.

33
00:01:52.067 --> 00:01:56.547
Why? With a partial block, a silent patch sits next to active patches.

34
00:01:56.967 --> 00:01:59.627
With a full block, every site is equally silent.

35
00:02:00.227 --> 00:02:01.887
Nothing is less active than its neighbor.

36
00:02:02.507 --> 00:02:07.607
Active sites can destabilize inactive synapses nearby, over a short distance within

37
00:02:07.607 --> 00:02:09.587
one junction. Keep this rule.

38
00:02:10.147 --> 00:02:12.547
A less active synapse is more likely to go.
